<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José de Escandón</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1700">1700</a>–<a href="/years/1770">1770</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José de Escandón was a Spanish military officer and colonial administrator commissioned by the viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> in 1746 to colonise the Gulf coast north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampico">Tampico</a>. From 1748 he founded more than twenty towns and missions between the Pánuco and Nueces rivers, creating the province of Nuevo Santander, the later Mexican state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-de-escandon.jpg" alt="José de Escandón" width="320" height="517" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_de_Escand%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Escandón was born on 19 May 1700 at Soto de la Marina in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>, one of three sons of Juan de Escandón and Francisca de la Helguera, and came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> as a child in 1715. He volunteered as a cadet in the cavalry of Mérida, fought the English at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laguna-de-terminos">Laguna de Términos</a> and, promoted to lieutenant, was posted to Querétaro, where he fought in the wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apaches</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1727 he put down a revolt of the Pames in Celaya and became sergeant major of his regiment; he subdued rebels at the mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> in 1732, at Irapuato the next year, and 10,000 Indian rebels at San Miguel el Grande in 1734. Promoted to colonel, he became aide to the captain general of the Sierra Gorda, reformed the administration of its missions and fought the Tamaulipecos raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuevo-leon">Nuevo León</a>.</p><p>Worried by French encroachment from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> and English activity along the coast, the authorities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> resolved to complete the conquest of the Seno Mexicano, and a council of war held from 8 to 13 May 1748 chose Escandón's plan as the most complete. He proposed the name Nuevo Santander and a budget of 115,000 pesos, plus 500 pesos for each colonist, and the call for settlers quickly drew enough. From the founding of Llera on 25 December 1748 to 1755 he founded Santander, Soto la Marina, Güemes, Camargo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reynosa">Reynosa</a>, Mier and Revilla south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laredo">Laredo</a> north of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As governor he assigned activities and crops to each settlement, promoted irrigation and built roads, bridges and a mansion at Santander known as the Palace of the Count, and the Crown made him conde de Sierra Gorda. Known also as the Exterminator of the Pames of Querétaro, he was accused of murdering Indians and taking slaves and was tried for using Indian labour in his textile mills and bringing English contraband through Santander; his great wealth came from badly paid Indians and slaves and from land grants. He died forgotten in 1770 and was exonerated five years later. Ciudad Victoria raised a statue to him in 1975.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José de Escandón, 1st Count of Sierra Gorda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1746 the viceroy commissioned <b>José de Escandón</b> to colonise it, and from 1748 he founded over twenty towns and missions between the Pánuco and Nueces rivers, including Camargo, Reynosa and, in 1755, Laredo.</blockquote><a href="/state/nuevo-santander">Nuevo Santander</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Santa Cruz de Bezana, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.44,-3.9&amp;year=1745" aria-label="Santa Cruz de Bezana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4456 623 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of José de Escandón"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4906 893h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4906 893h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.44,-3.9&amp;year=1745">What was here in 1745? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
